The right fit is the Composite API, not sObject Collections or the Batch API. A composite request with `allOrNone: false` lets each order succeed or fail independently, but it doesn't make an order atomic on its own. To get per-order atomicity, use the **sObject Tree**? No, that is all-or-nothing for the whole call and can't upsert.

The better fit is **Composite Graph** (`/composite/graph`). Each graph is committed atomically: if any request in the graph fails, that whole graph is rolled back, while other graphs in the same call are unaffected. So one graph per order gives "saved completely or not at all" per order, and one failing order does not block the others. Graph IDs identify the units of work (the order numbers), in the order listed.

Within a graph, each composite request has a `url`, `method`, `referenceId`, and `body`. The account is upserted with a PATCH to `/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/C-1001`. The opportunity's `AccountId` references the account result with `@{acct_1001.id}`. An upsert returns the id in its response body for both create (201) and update (in recent versions, 200 returns the id too in composite), so the reference works.

The Customer_Number__c value is in the URL, so it is not included in the body.

