public inherited sharing class OpportunitiesSelector extends fflib_SObjectSelector {
    public Schema.SObjectType getSObjectType() {
        return Opportunity.SObjectType;
    }

    public List<Schema.SObjectField> getSObjectFieldList() {
        return new List<Schema.SObjectField>{
            Opportunity.Id,
            Opportunity.Name,
            Opportunity.AccountId,
            Opportunity.StageName,
            Opportunity.Amount,
            Opportunity.CloseDate
        };
    }

    public List<Opportunity> selectById(Set<Id> opportunityIds) {
        return (List<Opportunity>) selectSObjectsById(opportunityIds);
    }

    public List<Opportunity> selectTopOpenByAccountIds(Set<Id> accountIds, Integer maxRecords) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty() || maxRecords == null || maxRecords < 1) {
            return new List<Opportunity>();
        }

        fflib_QueryFactory queryFactory = newQueryFactory();
        queryFactory.selectField('Account.Name');
        queryFactory.setCondition('IsClosed = false AND AccountId IN :accountIds');
        queryFactory.setOrdering(
            Opportunity.Amount,
            fflib_QueryFactory.SortOrder.DESCENDING,
            true
        );
        queryFactory.addOrdering(
            Opportunity.CloseDate,
            fflib_QueryFactory.SortOrder.ASCENDING,
            false
        );
        queryFactory.setLimit(maxRecords);

        return (List<Opportunity>) Database.query(queryFactory.toSOQL());
    }
}
```

Note: in `fflib_QueryFactory`, the third parameter of `setOrdering`/`addOrdering` with a SortOrder is `nullsLast`, so `true` on Amount gives `NULLS LAST` and `false` on CloseDate gives `NULLS FIRST`, which is harmless since CloseDate is always populated.

Notes on the fflib_SObjectSelector API in recent versions:

- The constructors are `fflib_SObjectSelector()`, `(Boolean includeFieldSetFields)`, `(Boolean, Boolean enforceCRUD, Boolean enforceFLS)`, `(Boolean, Boolean, Boolean, Boolean sortSelectFields)`, and in newer versions `(Boolean includeFieldSetFields, fflib_SObjectSelector.DataAccess level)`. The data access setting is applied through `setDataAccess(fflib_QueryFactory.DataAccess level)`, with `fflib_QueryFactory.DataAccess.USER_MODE`.
- Field set fields are added by overriding `getSObjectFieldSetList()`, which returns `List<Schema.FieldSet>`. The selector includes them only when `includeFieldSetFields` is true, so the constructor must pass true.
- The no-arg constructor must therefore call `super(true)` and then set user mode. I'll define the no-arg constructor explicitly, and avoid the older CRUD/FLS flags so they don't conflict with user mode.
- For the child subquery, `addQueryFactorySubselect(fflib_QueryFactory parent, String relationshipName)` on the child selector returns the subquery factory. The parent query is built with `newQueryFactory()`, and the subquery is added with `new ContactsSelector().addQueryFactorySubselect(qf, 'Contacts')`. The `ContactsSelector` is `inherited sharing` with default constructors, so its own settings don't matter for the subselect. The subquery is part of the parent query, so user mode applies to the whole query.
- To run the query, use `Database.query(qf.toSOQL())`. A plain `Database.query` would not apply user mode, but `toSOQL()` of the factory appends `WITH USER_MODE` when the factory's data access is set. In `fflib_SObjectSelector.newQueryFactory()`, the selector's data access level is passed to the factory, so `toSOQL()` includes it. Executing the string with `Database.query` is therefore correct.
- Sharing: the class should be `inherited sharing`. User mode enforces sharing regardless.

For `selectById`, `selectSObjectsById` uses `newQueryFactory()` and `Database.query`, so it works with user mode.

