public without sharing class AccountRatingService {

    private static final Decimal HOT_THRESHOLD = 100000;

    public static void recalculate(Id accountId) {
        if (accountId == null) {
            return;
        }
        recalculate(new Set<Id>{ accountId });
    }

    // Single entry point: one async call for the whole batch.
    public static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }
        if (System.isFuture() || System.isBatch()) {
            // Future methods cannot be called from these contexts.
            applyRatings(accountIds);
        } else {
            recalculateAsync(accountIds);
        }
    }

    @future
    private static void recalculateAsync(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        applyRatings(accountIds);
    }

    private static void applyRatings(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        Map<Id, Decimal> maxAmount = new Map<Id, Decimal>();
        Set<Id> hasOpen = new Set<Id>();

        for (AggregateResult ar : [
            SELECT AccountId acc, MAX(Amount) maxAmt, COUNT(Id) cnt
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds AND IsClosed = false
            GROUP BY AccountId
        ]) {
            Id accId = (Id) ar.get('acc');
            hasOpen.add(accId);
            maxAmount.put(accId, (Decimal) ar.get('maxAmt'));
        }

        List<Account> toUpdate = new List<Account>();
        for (Account a : [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]) {
            String target;
            if (!hasOpen.contains(a.Id)) {
                target = 'Cold';
            } else {
                Decimal amt = maxAmount.get(a.Id);
                target = (amt != null && amt >= HOT_THRESHOLD) ? 'Hot' : 'Warm';
            }
            if (a.Rating != target) {
                a.Rating = target;
                toUpdate.add(a);
            }
        }
        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

I'd deviate from the instruction to call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` inside the loop. That method runs a SOQL query on every call. With up to 200 lines per opportunity, a loop would hit the 100-query synchronous governor limit and throw `System.LimitException`. A single CPQ insert would fail.

The fix keeps the rules in one place without editing `PricingRules` or copying its rules. The handler collects the product IDs, runs one query for their `Family` values, and calls the existing `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)` for each product. The discount values stay in `PricingRules`. The handler only fetches the family data in bulk.

Other details:
- Only lines with `Discount == null` are changed, so an explicit 0 is kept.
- Lines with a null `Product2Id` are skipped. `OpportunityLineItem` normally gets its product through the `PricebookEntry`, so `Product2Id` may be empty on insert. The handler therefore also resolves `PricebookEntryId` to a product in one bulk query. This is a safety net.
- The handler is `with sharing`, and the queries are bulk-safe (two at most, regardless of line count).
- Both queries use `WITH USER_MODE`? I left it out. Pricing lookups should work for any rep, and the sharing declaration already applies to record access. A rep without Product2 visibility would otherwise get no discount.

