```apex
public with sharing class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {
    private final Set<Id> caseIds;

    public CaseSurveyJob(Set<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = caseIds == null ? new Set<Id>() : new Set<Id>(caseIds);
    }

    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseIds = new Set<Id>();
        if (caseId != null) {
            this.caseIds.add(caseId);
        }
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (caseIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Case> cases = [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId, IsClosed
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id IN :caseIds
            WITH SYSTEM_MODE
        ];

        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        for (Case c : cases) {
            if (c.IsClosed && c.ContactId != null) {
                tasks.add(new Task(
                    WhatId = c.Id,
                    WhoId = c.ContactId,
                    Subject = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber,
                    ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(2),
                    Status = 'Not Started'
                ));
            }
        }

        if (tasks.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Partial success: one failing record does not block the others.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasks, false);
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                for (Database.Error err : results[i].getErrors()) {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'CSAT task failed for case ' + tasks[i].WhatId + ': ' + err.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The main problem with this approach is the `Messaging.sendEmail` call inside the loop. Apex limits you to 10 `sendEmail` invocations per transaction (the "Total number of sendEmail methods allowed" limit). With up to 200 opportunities per call, a loop with one call each would throw `System.LimitException: Too many Email Invocations: 11` on the 11th opportunity. A LimitException can't be caught, so the transaction would abort after at most 10 emails, and every later owner would be skipped.

The limit applies to invocations, not to messages. The fix is to build all messages in the loop and make one `Messaging.sendEmail(messages, false)` call after it. With `allOrNone=false`, a failed email doesn't block the others, which gives the isolation you wanted. The returned `SendEmailResult[]` lets me count successes.

On your note about allocation: single emails sent to internal users by `targetObjectId` don't count against the external daily limit. They are still subject to the 10-invocation limit and to the 5,000 per-day limit for mass mail, which doesn't apply to single emails, so 200 per call is fine.

Other details:
- `setTargetObjectId` with a User Id requires `setSaveAsActivity(false)`, which you already have.
- Query the opportunities in bulk, not per record, using `WITH USER_MODE`. Owner access is through `OwnerId`.
- Inactive owners can't receive email. I skip them, so they don't count as sent.
- Close date is formatted with `format()`.
- Null or empty input returns 0.

