I changed your approach. Loading every contact into a map with `[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]` would fail in this org. With about 2.3 million contacts, the query would hit the 50,000-row SOQL limit, and even if it didn't, the heap limit would be exceeded. It would also break on the first batch of 200 accounts.

Instead, the handler first collects only the accounts whose `BillingCountry` actually changed, keeping their old values in a map. It then runs one query, filtered by `AccountId IN :changedIds`, so only the relevant contacts come back. That is still one query with no queries in loops.

The query also filters on `MailingCountry` being blank or equal to one of the old countries. That keeps the result set to just the contacts that will be updated. The exact match against each account's own old value is rechecked in Apex, since the SOQL filter only narrows by the set of old values.

A few more details:
- The query is a `for` loop over the SOQL result, so records are processed in chunks of 200 and heap use stays low.
- Contacts are updated in one DML statement. If an account with many contacts exceeded 10,000 DML rows, a Batch or Queueable job would be needed, so I note that limit here. The `AccountId IN` query can return up to 50,000 rows per transaction.
- When the old `BillingCountry` was blank, the condition reduces to blank contacts only, so that case needs no special handling.
- Updating contacts will fire any Contact triggers, which is expected.
- The old-country set contains only non-blank values, because blank is handled by the separate `MailingCountry = null` clause.
- If the new `BillingCountry` is blank, contacts are set to blank. This follows the spec literally.

