The bypass API for a single action is `MetadataTriggerHandler.bypass(String actionName)`. It takes the Apex class name, so `'TA_Contact_CreateWelcomeTask'` is the right argument. `TriggerBase.bypass(sObjectName)` would skip all Contact automation, which isn't wanted here, so I avoided it.

I wrapped the insert in try/finally so the bypass is cleared even if the DML throws. `clearBypass` removes only this action. `clearAllBypasses` would also remove bypasses set by a caller earlier in the transaction, so I avoided it.

If the caller had already bypassed the action, an unconditional `clearBypass` would wrongly re-enable it. I check `isBypassed` first and restore the prior state. DML is a single statement, and a null or empty list is handled up front.

The insert uses default `insert` semantics, so any failure throws. The class is declared `with sharing`, which is the safer default for production code.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/ContactImportService.cls
```apex
public with sharing class ContactImportService {
    private static final String WELCOME_TASK_ACTION = 'TA_Contact_CreateWelcomeTask';

    public static void importContacts(List<Contact> contacts) {
        if (contacts == null || contacts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Boolean wasAlreadyBypassed = MetadataTriggerHandler.isBypassed(WELCOME_TASK_ACTION);
        MetadataTriggerHandler.bypass(WELCOME_TASK_ACTION);
        try {
            insert contacts;
        } finally {
            if (!wasAlreadyBypassed) {
                MetadataTriggerHandler.clearBypass(WELCOME_TASK_ACTION);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Design notes:

- The bypass is `TriggerBase.bypass('Account')`, which covers the whole Account sObject. `MetadataTriggerHandler.bypass` is for individual actions, so it is the wrong API here.
- `clearBypass('Account')` runs in a `finally` block. This is safe because the Account bypass is not already active when we start. If a caller had already bypassed Account, clearing it would break their intent, so I check `isBypassed` first and only clear it if we set it.
- The Contact update must not be bypassed. Contact has its own bypass flag, so the Account bypass does not affect it.
- Contact triggers may touch Accounts, and the Account bypass is still active during the Contact DML. That is acceptable because the requirement says the bypass covers the whole Account sObject during `reassign`, and Account automation resumes only after it finishes. I keep the bypass active across both DML statements, which is the safest reading.
- Queries: one SOQL on Contacts, with no query on Accounts. Accounts are built as `new Account(Id = id, OwnerId = newOwnerId)`, which gives one query and two DML statements.
- Contacts are queried with `AccountId IN :accountIds`.
- Null or empty input returns early, and a null owner is also guarded.
- DML is all-or-none: `update records;` throws on failure and propagates unchanged, and there is no catch block.
- Sharing: `with sharing` is the safe default for a service that changes ownership.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/AccountOwnershipService.cls
```apex
public with sharing class AccountOwnershipService {
    public static void reassign(Set<Id> accountIds, Id newOwnerId) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty() || newOwnerId == null) {
            return;
        }

        List<Account> accountsToUpdate = new List<Account>();
        for (Id accountId : accountIds) {
            accountsToUpdate.add(new Account(Id = accountId, OwnerId = newOwnerId));
        }

        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : [
            SELECT Id
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
            WITH USER_MODE
        ]) {
            contactsToUpdate.add(new Contact(Id = c.Id, OwnerId = newOwnerId));
        }

        Boolean alreadyBypassed = TriggerBase.isBypassed('Account');
        if (!alreadyBypassed) {
            TriggerBase.bypass('Account');
        }
        try {
            update accountsToUpdate;
            if (!contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
                update contactsToUpdate;
            }
        } finally {
            if (!alreadyBypassed) {
                TriggerBase.clearBypass('Account');
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Notes on the approach:

- `TriggerTestUtility.getFakeId(Schema.SObjectType)` takes an SObjectType, so I call it with `Opportunity.SObjectType`.
- `ERROR_MESSAGE` is `@TestVisible private static`, so the test can reference it as `TA_Opportunity_GuardAmountDecrease.ERROR_MESSAGE`.
- The old and new records share the same Id, because the action builds a map from `triggerOld` keyed by Id.
- `TriggerTestUtility` is an `@IsTest` class from the package, so it can be called from test code.
- With no namespace, no prefix is needed.
- `addError` on an in-memory record populates `hasErrors()` and `getErrors()` in tests. This works for records in a before-update context, and it also works for plain in-memory records.
- Records are created with `new Opportunity(Id = ..., Amount = ...)`.

