Design notes:

- The class implements `TriggerAction.BeforeInsert` and `TriggerAction.BeforeUpdate`.
- Candidates are contacts with a non-blank Email and an AccountId. On update, a contact is a candidate only if its Email or AccountId changed. Email changes are compared case-insensitively, which is safe because a case-only change still gets rechecked.
- Within-batch duplicates are tracked with a Set of `accountId|lowercase email` keys. The first candidate in the list claims its key and later ones are rejected. Contacts that are not candidates on update (unchanged legacy duplicates) are not added to the batch set. They are still in the database, so the query finds them.
- One SOQL query runs only if there are candidates. It filters on `AccountId IN :accountIds AND Email IN :emails`, which is a superset, and each row is then checked precisely in Apex. SOQL email comparison is case-insensitive, so mixed-case matches are found. The query excludes the candidate's own Id by checking in Apex (`Map<String, Set<Id>>` of existing contact Ids per key).
- A contact is rejected if any existing record with its key has an Id different from its own. On insert Id is null, so any existing match rejects it.
- Edge case: a candidate is rejected in-batch, but an earlier accepted candidate in the batch, which is itself being moved, may leave the DB state stale. The database still holds the old values, so a conflict with the old value of a contact being changed in the same batch could be a false positive. To handle this, existing rows whose Id is in the batch and which are themselves candidates are ignored, because their new values are what count. Rows for batch contacts that are not candidates keep their DB values, which equal their new values, so they are still counted.
- Also, a non-candidate contact in the batch (unchanged) with the same key as a candidate is a real conflict. The DB query catches this since its row is unchanged.
- The query uses the default sharing of the trigger context. The class is `inherited sharing`; to see all contacts regardless of visibility, `without sharing` would be more accurate for a uniqueness rule. I use `without sharing` for the query so the rule is enforced across contacts the user cannot see.
- The metadata records use the `Before_Insert__c` / `Before_Update__c` relationship to `Contact`.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/TA_Contact_PreventDuplicateEmail.cls
```apex
public without sharing class TA_Contact_PreventDuplicateEmail implements TriggerAction.BeforeInsert, TriggerAction.BeforeUpdate {
    private static final String MESSAGE = 'Another contact on this account already uses this email address.';

    public void beforeInsert(List<SObject> triggerNew) {
        check((List<Contact>) triggerNew, null);
    }

    public void beforeUpdate(List<SObject> triggerNew, List<SObject> triggerOld) {
        Map<Id, Contact> oldMap = new Map<Id, Contact>((List<Contact>) triggerOld);
        check((List<Contact>) triggerNew, oldMap);
    }

    private static String key(Id accountId, String email) {
        return (String) accountId + '|' + email.trim().toLowerCase();
    }

    private void check(List<Contact> newList, Map<Id, Contact> oldMap) {
        List<Contact> candidates = new List<Contact>();
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        Set<String> emails = new Set<String>();
        Set<Id> candidateIds = new Set<Id>();

        for (Contact c : newList) {
            if (c.AccountId == null || String.isBlank(c.Email)) {
                continue;
            }
            if (oldMap != null) {
                Contact o = oldMap.get(c.Id);
                if (o != null && o.AccountId == c.AccountId && o.Email == c.Email) {
                    continue;
                }
            }
            candidates.add(c);
            accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            emails.add(c.Email.trim());
            if (c.Id != null) {
                candidateIds.add(c.Id);
            }
        }
        if (candidates.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Set<String> existingKeys = new Set<String>();
        for (Contact e : [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, Email
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds AND Email IN :emails
        ]) {
            if (candidateIds.contains(e.Id) || String.isBlank(e.Email)) {
                continue;
            }
            existingKeys.add(key(e.AccountId, e.Email));
        }

        Set<String> seen = new Set<String>();
        for (Contact c : candidates) {
            String k = key(c.AccountId, c.Email);
            if (existingKeys.contains(k) || seen.contains(k)) {
                c.addError(MESSAGE);
            } else {
                seen.add(k);
            }
        }
    }
}
